Semantic Search :
    Document Loader : Document loaders provide standard interface for reading data from different sources like slack, notion or GDrive into Langchain's Document format. This ensures that the data can be handled consistently regardless of the source. Supports both 'Load all docs at once' and 'stream docs lazily from big ass datasets'

    Embedding : The process of turning Plain text to vectors.

    Vector Store : A specialized DB used to store numerical vector embeddings.

    Example : Let's work on a PDF Parsing
    
        * Loading the document : we need 2 npm packages '@langchain/community' and 'pdf-parse'
            $ npm install @langchain/community pdf-parse
            
        * How the documents will be formatted :
            >> import {Document} from '@langchain
            >> const documents = [
            >>    new Document({
            >>        pageContent:"Dogs are a great companions, known for their loyalty and friendliness.",
            >>        metadata   :{source:"mammal-pets-doc"},
            >>    }),
            >>    new Document({
            >>        pageContent:"Cats are independent pets that often enjoy their own space."
            >>        metadata   :{source:"mammal-pets-doc"},
            >>    }),
            >> ]
        
        * Parsing a PDF and creating langchain readable docs like the above : Mind You, This just Reads what's in the PDF.
            >> import {PDFLoader} from '@langchain/community/document_loaders/fd/pdf';
            >> const loader = new PDFLoader('./some.pdf');
            >> const docs   = await loader.load();
            >> console.log(docs);
            >> console.log(docs.length);
            
        * Splitting text in chunks of 1000 chars and creating an overlap of 200 chars :
            >> import {RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter} from '@langchain/textsplitters';
            >> const textSplitter = new RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter({
            >>    chunkSize : 1000,
            >>    chunkOverlap : 200,
            >> });
            >> const allSplits = await textSplitter.splitDocuments(docs);
            >> console.log(allSplits.length);
            >> console.log(allSplits);
            
