        >> import {SystemMessage} from '@langchain/core/messages';
        >> const tools = [retrieve];
        >> const systemPrompt = new SystemMessage(
                                    "You have access to a tool that retrieves context from a blog post. "+
                                    "Use the tool to help answer the user queries."
        >>                      );
        >> cosnt agent = createAgent({model:"gpt-5",tools,systemPrompt});

        * Testing the agent : 

        >> let inputMessage = `What is the standard method for Task Decomposition? Once you get the answer, look up common extensions of that method.`;

        >> let agentInputs  = {
        >>                      messages: [
        >>                          {role:"user",content:inputMessage},
        >>                      ]
        >>                    }
       
        >> const stream     = await agent.stream(agentInputs, {streamModel:"values",});

        >> for await (const step of stream) {
        >>      const lastMessage = step.messages[step.messages.length - 1];
        >>      console.log(`[${lastMessage.role}] : ${lastMessage.content}`);
        >>      console.log("------\n");
        >> };

        * This agent : 
            * Generates a query to search for a standard method for task decomposition.
            * Receiving the answer, generates a second query to search to common extension of it.
            * Having received all necessary context, answer the question.

    * RAG Chain : 
        * Agentic RAG:
            * The one we created above.
            * Decides when to search the VectorDB.
            * Decides whether to search the vectorDB or use the tool.
            * How many times to search.
        * When it comes to RAG chains, there's no decision-making.
        * It always takes the user query, runs a vector search no matter what, injects the results into the prompt, calls the llm once.
        * RAG Chains runs a vector search even if the user says 'hello'.
        * So, basically everytime users asks for anything. We pack the whole relevant docs and send it to llm.
        * Good news is there will only be one inference call. Bad news is low latency.
        * Example : 
            >> import {createAgent,dynamicSystemPromptMiddleware} from 'langchain';
            >> import { SystemMessage } from '@langchain/core/messages';
            >> const agent = createAgent({
            >>          model,
            >>          tools: [],
            >>          middleware : [
            >>              dynamicSystemPromptMiddleware(async (state) => {
            >>                  const lastQuery = state.message[state.message.length - 1].content;
            >>                  const retrievedDocs = await vectorStore.similaritySearch(lastQuery,2);
            >>                  const docsContent = retrievedDocs.map(doc => doc.pageContent).join('\n\n');
            >>                  const systemMessage = new systemMessage(
            >>                                          `You are a helpful assistant. Use the following context in your response: \n\n${docsContent}`
            >>                                        );
            >>                  return [systemMessage,...state.messages];
            >>              });
            >>          ];
            >> });

